# react-testing (Cursor)

Test what the user sees and does, not implementation details. A test
should render with the same providers as production, interact via
accessible queries + `userEvent`, and assert visible output/observable
side effects. It should NOT inspect component state/props/hooks-called, or
mock React itself.

## Runner choice

Pick one: Vitest (Vite/Remix/modern), Jest (Next.js/CRA/established
repos), or Playwright/Cypress Component Testing when JSDOM lacks a needed
feature. Don't run RTL+Vitest and Playwright CT in the same repo without a
clear lane separation.

## Query priority

Top-down: accessible queries first (`getByRole`, `getByLabelText`,
`getByText`), then semantic (`getByAltText`), `getByTestId` as a last
resort. `getBy*` throws on no match, `queryBy*` returns null (assert
absence), `findBy*` is async (elements appearing after async work).

## Interaction and async

Always `await` `userEvent` calls; call `userEvent.setup()` once per test.
For async UI: `findBy*`, `waitFor`, `waitForElementToBeRemoved` — never
`setTimeout` + assertion.

## Network mocking

MSW (`setupServer`) with `onUnhandledRequest: 'error'` so an unmocked
request fails loudly. Override per-test with `server.use(...)`.

## Provider wrapping

Centralize a `renderWithProviders` helper in `test-utils.tsx` wrapping
QueryClient/Theme/Router providers once, imported by every test file.

## Custom hooks

`renderHook` + `act` for state-changing calls; test through the hook's
public API only; instantiate a `QueryClient` once outside the wrapper
closure (inside it resets cache every render → flaky tests).

## Accessibility

Run `axe` against every interactive component
(`expect(await axe(container)).toHaveNoViolations()`). Catches missing
labels, invalid ARIA, missing alt text, heading-order violations — but
JSDOM has no real CSS engine, so visual contrast still needs Playwright.

## Snapshots

Avoid for rendered component output (breaks on every style change, gets
rubber-stamped, tests DOM structure not behavior). Fine for pure data
serialization functions or generated config output.

## When to reach for Playwright/Cypress instead

JSDOM can't do real layout, CSS transitions, drag-and-drop, iframes,
cross-origin, or real network with DevTools. A hook/presentational
component/logic-bearing form → RTL. A component whose layout matters or
uses unsupported browser APIs → Playwright CT. A full multi-page user flow
→ full E2E (`e2e-testing`).

## Anti-patterns

`container.querySelector` bypassing accessible queries; asserting render
count; mocking React itself; mocking child components by default; ignoring
`act()` warnings; shared mutable state across tests; a test that still
passes with `it.skip()` removed.
